00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.210
in terms of the
[INAUDIBLE] experience.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.550
So let's talk about serverless.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.510
I know you didn't come here to
hear about serverless, did you?

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.570
So you might think, hey,
I have on-prem stuff.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.363
Maybe I want to do
multi-cloud stuff.

00:01:39.363 --> 00:01:41.030
Why are you telling
me about serverless?

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.660
And the reason for that
is serverless world

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.390
has been taking the
internet by the storm,

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.110
because they have done something
that is pretty remarkable.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.050
I can write my application
code, and I can directly

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.520
deploy it without
knowing anything

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.218
about the infrastructure
whatsoever.

00:01:57.218 --> 00:01:59.010
So this might be totally
irrelevant to you.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.870
If you heard about Google
Cloud Functions, Lambda,

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.390
and these sort of things, these
sort of technologies, maybe

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.940
this is very different than
what you've been already doing.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.160
But there's something
that we can learn from.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.410
And the fact that
your developers do not

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.000
care about the
infrastructure anymore,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.250
and they can be productive,
they can write code and go

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.720
to production in a very
short amount of time,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.910
I think that's
pretty remarkable.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.940
So when we think
about serverless,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.160
we're seeing that there's
an operational model that

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.040
makes serverless serverless,
and there's a programming model.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.915
So first of all, there
is no infra management.

00:02:31.915 --> 00:02:34.380
In the serverless world,
they have this place,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.950
they don't touch
machines anymore,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.520
they don't configure load
balancers, firewalls.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.000
And just because the
infrastructure under them

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.770
updates regularly and
it's patched regularly,

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:46.770
they just write their
code, and everything else,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.990
their dependencies,
the OS packages,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.880
and the runtimes,
everything, it gets

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.655
patched by the cloud provider.

00:02:52.655 --> 00:02:54.280
So there's a world
like that out there.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.355
And they pay-per-use.

00:02:55.355 --> 00:02:56.730
For example, you
got one request,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:57.910
you pay for one request.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:58.830
That's it.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.410
And similarly, they've been
constructing their applications

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.500
as little microservices or
little functions, lambdas.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.060
And these applications
are invoked

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.700
through events or requests
that come to that application.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.910
So they've been
writing these services.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.460
And I'm assuming
some of you also have

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.000
services that sound like that.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.460
So at the end, I think we can
learn something from this.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.410
So serverless, at
the end of the day,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.200
is nothing more than
efficient developers

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.640
and efficient operators.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.240
This is what it's
bringing developers

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.390
to these sort of platforms.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.340
If you look at it,
like, Google App Engine

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.900
was released a decade ago.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:34.620
That's not new.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.172
We've been doing serverless
for a pretty long time.

00:03:37.172 --> 00:03:38.880
And the reason people
have been opting in

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.190
for that model is they're
pretty efficient at it.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.370
They can write their code, go
to the production or staging

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.750
in a minute or so.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.330
And this is precisely
why I think there is

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:49.610
something to learn from that.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.360
So developers care
about velocity.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.900
They want to write their code.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.350
They want to take their code
and put it into production,

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.368
and they want to do
it in a reliable way.

00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:03.910
When they ship their
code, they don't

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.490
want to hear from their
operators or DevOps

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.040
person or the [INAUDIBLE]
that they broke everything.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.540
So they want that
reproducibility.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.650
They want that monitoring.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.480
They want these pieces that
makes the whole thing reliable.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.149
So this is where we
introduce Cloud Run.

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:19.950
So Cloud Run, as
of last week, it's

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.480
a generally available product.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.250
It's Google's
serverless platform

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.010
that lets you run any container
image in Google's fully

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.253
managed infrastructure.

00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:28.920
You give us a container
image, let's say

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.390
there's a web server
in that image,

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.887
and that's the end of the story.

00:04:31.887 --> 00:04:32.970
We run that image for you.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.340
We give you an endpoint
that is fully secured.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.570
We scale it for you.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.310
You call Google,
you say, I'm going

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.290
to have a Black
Friday sales coming up

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.140
tomorrow, or in a week or so.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.710
Can you schedule me?

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.110
Can you allocate something
like this to me, that is,

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.510
like, maybe I want
5,000 containers?

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.830
And then Google tells you,
OK, you're good to go.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:54.090
And then next thing you
know, Black Friday, 9:00 AM,

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.990
you're getting hundreds
of thousands of requests,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.940
and Cloud Run can effectively
scale that for you.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.350
And we're going to talk
about how that looks like.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.415
So as I talked
earlier, Cloud Run,

00:05:04.415 --> 00:05:06.540
you come with a container
image that you're already

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.123
familiar with.

00:05:07.123 --> 00:05:08.550
That's the price of entry.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.480
As long as you can package your
application into a container,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.950
you can deploy to Cloud Run.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.080
And you can run any
language and framework.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.840
So this is how it differs
than most other serverless

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.690
platforms, because normally,
when we think of functions

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:25.350
and app engine paradigms, we're
restricted to certain languages

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.120
and their versions.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.690
Normally, you can run Python
2.7 and 3.2 or something,

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.410
but you can't go beyond that.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.290
So Cloud Run gives
you that flexibility.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.880
And it's fully managed
infrastructure.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.250
And you pay for what you use.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.680
And you get rapid
autoscaling that effectively

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.190
handles traffic spikes.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:43.650
So you're still
probably thinking

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.820
this is irrelevant to me.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.110
This is not quite what I do.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.510
But anyway, I'm
going to show you how

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.060
Cloud Run looks like in action.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.810
Can we switch to the
demo machine, please?

