00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.880
So what if you want
these functionality

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.410
in your Kubernetes cluster?

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:32.560
So this was precisely
where Cloud Run on Anthos

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.700
comes into picture.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:35.290
So we want to give
you the choice

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.600
of where you want to have
this developer experience.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.058
Because as a developer, I can
just build a container image,

00:09:40.058 --> 00:09:43.120
and go to a pretty well
managed environment that

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.840
already simplifies a lot
of the operations for me.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.810
So this is where Cloud for
Anthos comes into the picture.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.310
It's the same
experience that you

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.110
get on a serverless platform.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.530
It runs in your GKE Kubernetes
cluster next to your Kubernetes

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:57.590
workloads.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:09:58.900
So this is still Kubernetes.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:00.850
You're still running
a Kubernetes cluster,

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.710
but you give your developers
a higher-level abstraction.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.950
And if you want to customize
all the autoscaling

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:09.700
and how the load balancing work,
this is pretty customizable,

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.540
and I'm going to
touch on that shortly.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.480
So your developers might be--

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.030
maybe they want a UI.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.580
So this is what I did.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:17.210
I used the UI.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:18.790
I could have used
the CLI as well.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.430
If you're into infrastructure
as code-- for example,

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:24.990
in the previous talk, you've
seen Vic and his friends demo

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:29.000
an application that is purely
deployed from a YAML file.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.130
So if I want that, I
can still go that route.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.590
I can write a YAML file,
and I can deploy that.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.800
And under the covers,
the way Cloud Run

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.600
and Cloud Run on Anthos
work the same way

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.020
is because they both use this
thing called Knative API.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.180
And Knative is that
open technology

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.390
that makes this possible
to run your applications

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.690
either on Google's fully managed
platform or in your clusters,

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:54.790
either on cloud or on-prem or
maybe another cloud provider.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:58.200
So Knative is that open
source API and implementation

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.010
that codifies these practices
of running microservices

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.960
or serverless applications
on Kubernetes.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.250
It's an open API.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.800
It is jointly developed
by Google, Red Hat, IBM,

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.070
and few others.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:13.020
So by adding Knative,
which is, in this case,

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:15.210
managed by Cloud Run on
Anthos to my cluster,

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.280
I'm able to run stateless
applications more effectively.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.510
I can go somewhere,
I can click a button,

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:22.560
and then that application
is pretty much managed

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.080
and autoscaled for
me at that point.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.290
I don't have to worry about
configuring Horizontal Pod

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.900
Autoscalers, memory-based
scaling, and these sort

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.590
of things anymore.

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:36.402
So in terms of autoscaling,
if you look at Kubernetes

00:11:36.402 --> 00:11:38.110
today, you're probably
writing all these,

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.330
like, Horizontal Pod
Autoscaler configurations

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:41.680
that are memory- and CPU-based.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:44.950
So actually, something needs
to happen, like, for example,

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.280
your CPU utilization
needs to hit 70%, 80%,

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:49.990
and then at that
point, you're going

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:51.550
to get autoscaling happen.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.660
But by then, it
might be too late.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:56.380
So in Knative case, we're
doing rapid request-based

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:57.150
autoscaling.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.380
Anytime a request
comes, that's an input

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.070
to the autoscaling machine.

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.490
So if I get suddenly
100 requests,

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.040
and I can't handle 100
requests, that actually

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.170
means that I need to
hold onto those requests

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:12.245
and add more pods, so that
I can send the traffic to.

00:12:12.245 --> 00:12:14.480
And the other thing
is scale to zero.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:15.890
Maybe you don't care about this.

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.380
But actually, if your
application is not running,

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.230
this gives you a greater
ability to fit more applications

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:22.340
into one cluster.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:25.850
If an application does not get
any requests, it's not running.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:29.000
Next time it gets the request,
we wake up the application.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.050
But at the same time, we
hold onto that request.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:32.595
We don't drop the request.

00:12:32.595 --> 00:12:34.220
So this is not
available in Kubernetes.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:36.750
And this is what you
get with Knative.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.050
And similarly,
normally in Kubernetes,

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:43.137
you have the connection-based,
like, TCP-based load balancing.

00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:44.970
I'm sure if you're using
this in production,

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.803
you probably realize
that's not the best way

00:12:46.803 --> 00:12:48.470
to do load balancing.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:52.250
And in Knative case, we do
per-request load balancing

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:53.220
and traffic splitting.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:56.150
So this lets you do [INAUDIBLE]
rollouts or blue/green

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:57.650
deployments.

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.770
And this is all possible
because in an Anthos cluster,

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.740
there is Anthos Service
Mesh providing load

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:08.360
balancing capabilities and
Stackdriver support that lets

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.557
you monitor your applications.

00:13:11.557 --> 00:13:13.890
So now we're going to go back
to the demo machine again.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.432
And let's try to
Cloud Run on Anthos,

00:13:15.432 --> 00:13:17.420
and how different
could it actually be.

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.300
So I think I'm going
to turn off my Wi-Fi.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.330
I'm hoping I'm connected
over the wire right now.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:31.210
Let's try this.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:32.710
It was a previous
application that I

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:33.820
was trying to connect to.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:35.770
I don't know why it's
still not working.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.290
But let's go ahead and
deploy the same application

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:41.460
in a Cloud Run on an
Anthos cluster this time.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.570
So I go here, and I type
the image name again.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:48.090
Yep.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.830
And then this time, I'm going
to say, let's choose a Cloud

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:51.858
Run on Anthos cluster.

00:13:51.858 --> 00:13:53.400
And in my project,
it turns out there

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:54.960
is a GKE cluster available.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:56.110
I choose that.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:59.130
And then I say, let anyone
connect to this application.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.680
Then I go ahead and
create the application.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.040
So again, the same thing.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:04.600
I use the same
developer experience.

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.760
As a developer,
this is what I see.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:07.810
I have an application.

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:09.860
I have maybe, like,
a memory CPU setting.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.130
But at the end of the day,
I just want to deploy it.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.860
And in a few seconds,
I get a URL back.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:15.957
I click on it.

00:14:15.957 --> 00:14:18.040
So this was the application
that you were supposed

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.380
to see in the first place.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.410
As you can see here, I'm able
to deploy the same application

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:24.890
to Cloud Run on Anthos.

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.040
But I told you earlier,
this is still Kubernetes.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.390
So let's go out and
take a look at that.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.488
So here, I'm going to
bring up my Cloud Shell.

00:14:31.488 --> 00:14:34.030
If you don't know that, that's
a pretty cool feature we have.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:39.460
So let's take a look at
my Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.840
Let's try to understand
what's going on here.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.130
And as you can see, I have a
three-node GKE cluster here.

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:48.290
And what I just deployed was
actually a Kubernetes object.

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:51.770
So for example, if I
close this down here,

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:53.630
if I look at the
YAML, well, this

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.370
looks a lot like
Kubernetes, right?

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:58.220
This is all these
specs and containers,

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:00.740
resources, memory limits.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.570
So now let's try to
actually make sense of this.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:09.750
So here, I'm going to ask
for an API type called--

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.090
usually, I'll get Kservice.

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:14.312
So Kservice is the Knative
service abstraction.

00:15:14.312 --> 00:15:16.270
It's not greatly named,
because there's already

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.420
a Kubernetes Service object.

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.620
But hold on for a moment.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:24.390
So it seems like I already have
the PDF application I deployed

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:25.980
in the cluster running.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.750
So I see the service here.

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:29.860
If I do the kubectl,
I'll get svc,

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.560
which is the normal
Kubernetes service.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.790
I can see that there's already
a service created for that PDF

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.140
application here.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.510
And essentially, it's
making the application

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:42.430
that I just deployed
available within my cluster.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:44.520
So if someone else wants
to call this application

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.740
within the same cluster,
they can do that.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.830
Maybe I can limit connectivity
to only the cluster.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:51.930
I can say, don't allow
anyone externally

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.250
calling this application.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:57.920
I can have a completely private
microservice deploy this way.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.230
So for example,
let's take a look

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.545
at the pods, what's
running in this cluster.

00:16:02.545 --> 00:16:04.670
So you're going to see that
there is a pod running,

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:05.990
but it's actually going away.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:06.853
It's terminating.

00:16:06.853 --> 00:16:08.270
And the reason
it's terminating is

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:11.450
because I didn't send it
any requests for the past 30

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:12.950
seconds or so.

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:17.170
So if I go back and refresh,
so right now, a new container

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:17.950
is coming up.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:20.030
It came up, and it
served the application.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.767
So if I go back and run
kubectl, I'll get pods again.

00:16:23.767 --> 00:16:25.725
As you can see here,
there's a new pod running.

00:16:25.725 --> 00:16:28.740
And those just started,
like, eight seconds ago.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.540
So this is sort of
how Knative works.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.040
Let's go back to
the slides, please.

00:16:39.308 --> 00:16:40.850
Can we go back to
the slides, please?

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:43.910
All right.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.640
So this is Cloud Run on
Anthos in a nutshell.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.450
So what I just did was I had
the same developer experience.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:53.540
I went from the UI,
which just told me, hey,

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:54.830
choose your container image.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:56.660
I specify my
container image, just

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.730
like I'm doing
serverless stuff, but I

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.350
was able to get this in
my Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.560
So we saw how Knative
works under the covers.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:07.160
It's still Kubernetes objects.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:10.220
Your DevOps people and
your administrators

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:13.099
and operations people can go
and configure these objects

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:13.760
further.

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.250
You can impose Kubernetes
policies on them.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.260
You can use Anthos
Config Management

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:21.380
to spread these objects or
apply policies that would

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:23.690
limit access to these objects.

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:25.670
Because this is
still Kubernetes.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.880
And I want to actually go
back to the demo machine

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.440
momentarily, because I forgot
to show the traffic splitting.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.560
So let's actually see that, too.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.620
I just deployed another
application here.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.650
It is called hello-app.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.690
So this is running a
similar application image.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.290
If I click on that, that's
going to come up shortly.

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:48.320
As you can see, this
is a Hello World 1.0.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.770
As a developer, I can say,
hey, deploy a new revision.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:53.912
For example, I get 2.0.

00:17:53.912 --> 00:17:57.440
And then I say, let's go
ahead and deploy this.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.420
And after I deploy
this application,

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.740
I can actually say,
let's manage the traffic.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:06.630
For example, I want to send
50% of the traffic to v2.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.440
So I go ahead and Save this.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.940
Again, this is possible because
we're using Anthos Service

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:12.120
Mesh under the covers.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.220
We get that load
balancer with Istio.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:16.290
We get the same load
balancing capabilities

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.320
that you would normally
get in an Anthos cluster.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.700
So I go back to the application.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:24.180
Now every time I refresh, I'm
either going to get v1 or v2.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.207
So refreshing, 1, 1, 1, 2.

00:18:26.207 --> 00:18:28.290
So that's pretty much how
traffic splitting works.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:31.082
If I were to set this to
a value like 5% or 10%,

00:18:31.082 --> 00:18:32.790
I would be able to do
[INAUDIBLE] rollout

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.740
or blue/green deployments.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:36.660
So this is pretty much
how traffic splitting

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.310
works in a nutshell.

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.810
Let's go back to
the slides, please.

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:43.740
Yeah.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.110
And finally, this
is still Kubernetes.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.020
We're not adding new complexity.

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:49.590
This is a software-based stack.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:51.780
I did load balancing
with software.

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:55.260
I configured a new
application with software.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:58.200
I didn't involve any
sort of hardware in this.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.410
I was able to use what's already
in your cluster, which is

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.090
the Anthos Service Mesh setup.

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:05.170
If I want to get metrics
out of this application,

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:07.160
this is still possible.

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:10.940
So now let's briefly talk about
migrating your applications

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:11.840
to Cloud Run.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:15.840
So what sort of applications
actually fit into Cloud Run?

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.510
So first of all,
your application

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:19.540
needs to be stateless.

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:21.610
A lot of services are,
it turns out, stateless.

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:24.290
Yes, there are stateful
services as well.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:27.520
But if you have microservices,
front ends, anything

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:30.110
that handles events
or processing queues,

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.440
these sort of applications,
actually, they're

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:33.160
fault tolerant.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:35.200
You can kill them, or
you can scale them up,

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:36.800
and they usually
pick up the load,

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:39.040
and they continue where
they left off, or something

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.800
like that, that helps
you build applications

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:42.670
that are resilient.

00:19:42.670 --> 00:19:45.090
So if you have an
application, you

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:48.130
can make it work on
an HTTP or gRPC port,

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:49.450
you can run it on Cloud Run.

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:51.550
And ideally, if it's
not an application that

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:53.140
takes 10 minutes to start--

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:55.780
as you see here, every
time I get any request,

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:59.340
I start a new instance of
that, and that instance

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:02.770
stays around, so that I can
send more requests to it.

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:05.320
But additionally, you
can configure the Knative

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:06.220
autoscaler.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.780
I can go and say,
hey, you know what?

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:09.470
I don't want cold starts.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.470
So that's the biggest
problem in serverless.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.600
People say, there's cold starts.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:14.680
I don't want cold starts.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.280
In that case, you
can say, please

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:18.880
scale my application
to maybe minimum one

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:20.198
instead of going to zero.

00:20:20.198 --> 00:20:21.490
You can say, I don't want zero.

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:22.730
I want to keep it one.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.240
So you can have
that on Cloud Run.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:29.230
So here, I have the usual
Kubernetes Deployment

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:31.390
and Service file.

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:33.242
They say, hey, I have
this application.

00:20:33.242 --> 00:20:33.950
Here's the image.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:35.557
Here's the CPU requirements.

00:20:35.557 --> 00:20:37.390
And then to make this
application accessible

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.170
internally to my cluster,
I define a service.

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:41.950
So let's go through
this and see if there

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:45.100
are commonalities
that we can read out.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.170
For example, replicas, 1.

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:48.770
I don't need
replicas, 1, because

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:51.220
with Cloud Run on
Anthos, I'm getting

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:52.460
autoscaling out of the box.

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:55.878
I don't need to worry about
how many replicas are running.

00:20:55.878 --> 00:20:57.170
And there are all these labels.

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.770
I don't know if anyone here
actually goes and configures

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:01.330
labels on their
deployments, so they

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:04.760
can choose them and have them
overlapping labels and stuff

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:05.260
like that.

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:06.850
So that doesn't
happen very often.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.380
And similarly, on
the Service side,

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.000
there are all these labels.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.760
And I'm trying to do
the selector, because I

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:14.230
need to go back
to the Deployment

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:17.047
and match to the policies
deployed by the Deployment.

00:21:17.047 --> 00:21:19.630
And you know, I'm still in the
business of doing port numbers.

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:21.130
This is pretty interesting.

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:23.290
With Cloud Run on
Anthos, you don't have

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:24.670
to do port numbers anymore.

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:26.290
The port number is given to you.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:27.460
And you can customize that.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:29.100
So there is that.

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.000
So why don't we get rid
of the Service object

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:34.280
altogether and take the good
parts of the Deployment?

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:37.410
So that gives us the new
Knative Service object.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.520
As you can see here,
it's pretty brief.

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.470
It only has a few lines.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:44.360
I can just specify
my deployment spec,

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:47.560
which contains the only critical
information about my container.

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:48.440
What is my image?

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:49.690
What are my resources?

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:52.090
If you need to add labels,
you can still go and do that,

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:53.710
but you don't have to.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.290
So compared to the
Deployment object,

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:57.820
I think we've come a long way.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:01.060
As you can see, pretty much
what I just changed was

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.730
I changed the API group,
and I took the parts that

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:05.480
mattered into the new object.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:08.046
And now I'm actually running
on Knative, just Cloud Run

00:22:08.046 --> 00:22:09.250
on Anthos.

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:10.795
So here I'm going
to transfer to Jay.

00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:12.670
And he's going to talk
to you about eventing,

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:14.670
which is a critical part
in serverless developer

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.934
experience.

00:22:16.934 --> 00:22:18.798
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.580
JAY SMITH: Thank you.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.550
I want to create a
data-driven organization.

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:32.630
Has anybody heard that before,
maybe in the field, maybe

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:34.880
their manager said that
at some point in time?

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:39.200
Who heard something about
data-driven organizations?

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:39.790
All right.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:41.165
Does that actually
mean anything?

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:47.400
It sounds like one
of those buzz terms.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:48.898
Oh, we're data-driven.

00:22:48.898 --> 00:22:49.940
We collect a lot of data.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:51.450
We like data.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:52.950
But what are we
doing with the data?

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:57.305
Are we just storing it in some
warehouse in the Nevada desert?

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:02.840
Is it actually being
used for something?

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:04.550
My theory is that
when people say

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:07.340
they want a data-driven
organization, what

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:11.840
they actually mean is they want
an event-driven organization.

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:14.210
They don't want to just
get arbitrary data coming

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:15.380
into their application.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:18.772
They actually want to know
that something is happening out

00:23:18.772 --> 00:23:20.730
there in the world that
their application needs

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.310
to respond to.

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:25.880
Maybe you have a rideshare
app, and somebody has just

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.700
ordered a ride, or
maybe there's traffic,

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:31.490
and you want to be able
to update the estimated

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:34.535
time of arrival.

00:23:34.535 --> 00:23:36.450
This data needs to
serve a purpose.

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:40.130
So when we start
framing data as events,

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:42.593
then it starts to
serve a better purpose

00:23:42.593 --> 00:23:44.510
for our application,
something we can actually

00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:47.050
build a solution towards.

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:48.800
And in fact, if you
saw one of the earlier

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.200
slides when we were
talking about serverless,

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:54.170
one of the six, I
guess you could say,

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:58.100
segments of serverless
was event-driven.

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:01.040
And how do we do that?

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:03.420
Well, Knative actually has
a component called Eventing.

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.070
So Cloud Run is based
on Knative Serving.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:07.940
Knative Eventing is
another component

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:11.780
that makes up this whole
Knative suite, if you will.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:14.900
Eventing is--
well, it allows you

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:18.740
to declaratively bind
between event producers

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:20.070
and deployed services.

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.480
So now you're able
to just create

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:26.420
a producer or a source that
creates the events, that

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.460
collects the events,
and then just

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:32.180
deploy services that
reads said source.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:34.070
You can scale from a few events.

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:37.940
Maybe you do a update
on your inventory

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:41.630
at the end of the day, or maybe
you want to do a livestream.

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:44.690
And then you can create custom
event pipelines to connect

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:47.220
with your own existing systems.

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:51.890
So perhaps you have some
kind of Kafka cluster

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:56.000
out there, something we're
going to talk about very soon.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.070
So Knative Eventing
allows you to do all this.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:24:59.630
This is actually
pretty important,

00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:01.047
because you might
be saying, well,

00:25:01.047 --> 00:25:04.910
what happens in that earlier
setup when we deployed?

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.160
It gave us a URL.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:09.650
Why don't I just kind
of turn on the fire hose

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:13.230
and send all of my
data to that endpoint?

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.090
That'll work, right?

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:15.590
Maybe.

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:20.095
You can actually
do a lot of things

00:25:20.095 --> 00:25:22.350
with Knative Eventing. here
you have a source, which

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:25.200
could be your container.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.110
It could be GitHub, maybe
something changed in GitHub.

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.720
It could be your Kafka cluster.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:32.700
It could be something
in Kubernetes itself,

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:36.080
and it can just go and deploy
to your service, which is fine.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:37.830
But what happens when
you want to fan out?

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:41.370
What happens and you want
certain services to receive

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:42.120
certain events?

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.640
What if you want
to define events?

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:46.950
Can't really do that as
easily when you're just

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.992
doing the straight HTTP.

00:25:48.992 --> 00:25:50.700
And then, of course,
how do you guarantee

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:54.100
the events are sent if
you're doing it that way?

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:57.510
So what Knative will allow you
to do is do a more complex--

00:25:57.510 --> 00:25:59.170
or Knative Eventing,
rather, will

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:03.690
allow you to do a more
complex serving of your events

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:05.610
rather than just a
turning on the fire hose,

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:12.820
so to speak, onto
a HTTPS endpoint.

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:14.680
An easier way to
look at it, and what

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:18.070
I'm going to be showing you,
is we have this Kafka cluster

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:19.360
out here.

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:20.620
We have a Knative source.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:23.500
Knative source, that
is essentially--

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.810
think of it as kind of
a bucket, if you will,

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:27.730
where all the events
go to, and that

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:32.140
serves as the source of all
truth for the events that

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:32.640
come in.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.890
Then the broker's kind of
another abstraction layer

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:38.450
that you can then push
the event to the app.

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:41.962
So the source just collects
that information, broker kind of

00:26:41.962 --> 00:26:43.420
gives it a little
more intelligent,

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:47.830
and then the app or service is
what actually functions there.

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:52.100
And it uses the
event to do whatever.

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:57.500
Simple idea in terms of the real
world is we have our user here.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:58.710
They have a mobile phone.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:03.500
They want to order a
pizza with their app.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:04.940
We use Confluent Cloud.

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.070
And Confluent is
actually founded

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.870
by the creators of Kafka.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.860
And they actually have
a fully managed solution

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.410
that you can host on GCP, too.

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:18.560
So you get open source Kafka,
all the benefits of open source

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:21.660
Kafka, plus an SLA in support.

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.180
So if you're an
enterprise, wouldn't it

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:27.090
be nice to have somebody
else worry about your Kafka

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.323
versus your developers?

00:27:30.323 --> 00:27:32.490
And then, of course, while
we're talking about that,

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:33.365
you have the Knative.

00:27:33.365 --> 00:27:35.650
So let's go ahead
and look at our demo.

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.980
I actually recorded
it, because I

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:41.940
wish I had the confidence
to do a live demo.

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:43.950
Well, you know, internet
Wi-Fi is what it is.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:44.803
But hey.

00:27:44.803 --> 00:27:46.170
Or hotel Wi-Fi.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.940
Before we start
playing the video,

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:51.090
just going to explain a little
bit about what you will see.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:55.355
I created a-- oop, there
it is, but it's small.

00:27:58.150 --> 00:28:02.170
Anyway, so what you will see is
I created a small little app.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:04.120
All it does is it
pings Alpha Vantage.

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:09.820
Alpha Vantage is a API tool
that essentially will give you

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:12.580
different financial data,
so stock markets, currency

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:13.720
exchange, all of that.

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:16.130
You get, like, 500
free calls a day.

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:19.140
So hey, it's perfect
if you're doing a demo.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:22.350
It will check, and then
it will send the results

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:26.760
to a Kafka cluster, and
then we will show you

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:28.600
my Knative Eventing.

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:30.300
Can we restart that real quick?

00:28:33.745 --> 00:28:35.370
Wish I could stop
myself from clicking.

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:37.020
So I'm in the Kafka cluster.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:38.940
I'm in my Kubernetes
cluster, rather.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:43.170
This all has Knative Eventing
and Cloud Run installed.

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:44.220
Here's my service.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:45.630
Look, there's Cloud Run.

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:46.900
There's currency-app.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:51.350
That's the application
I deployed on Cloud Run.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:53.630
Now we are looking--
oop, there's the API.

00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:55.650
There's what the URL is.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.275
That's my cluster I
showed you earlier.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:03.070
So I'm going to go
ahead and take that URL,

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:07.150
and go to Postman, and start
sending some API calls.

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:11.600
Now, this is my endpoint.

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:14.300
Basically, it takes
two currencies, USD

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:17.130
and Japanese yen.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:20.370
Now, because of hotel Wi-Fi
when I was recording it,

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.643
this took a little while.

00:29:22.643 --> 00:29:24.060
But what it's going
to be doing is

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:26.650
it's going to be comparing and
