00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:30.190
There you go.

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:32.190
So remember that number.

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:35.340
Now we're going to go,
and this is actually

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:36.960
consuming my Kafka cluster.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:39.180
So Kafka got that number.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:41.210
Now, what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:29:41.210 --> 00:29:42.900
to go ahead and get
all the pods that

00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:47.050
are running in that
namespace that I created.

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:51.010
Event display is kind of
a bucket, if you will,

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:56.670
that displays all the logs that
have been sent to the broker.

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:59.090
We're going to go ahead and
look at the logs, so kubectl.

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.007
We're going to go ahead
and look at those logs

00:30:01.007 --> 00:30:03.430
and see what we can find.

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:05.890
Now, this is using a format
called CloudEvents, which

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:11.920
is an open source
CNCF-built, I guess,

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.790
format for setting events.

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:15.730
So we're keeping
everything open source.

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:17.967
It stores the data in Base64.

00:30:17.967 --> 00:30:19.300
